<title>FINLAND: German gold plan no trick -- Duisenberg in paper.</title>
<headline>German gold plan no trick -- Duisenberg in paper.</headline>
<dateline>HELSINKI 1997-05-26</dateline>
<text>
<p>A decision by Germany's finance ministry to revalue its gold reserve in the run-up to EMU is &quot;not a trick&quot; but part of standardising national accounts, Dutch central bank head Wim Duisenberg was quoted as saying on Monday.</p>
<p>The comments from Duisenberg, scheduled in July to assume leadership of the European Monetary Institute, the forerunner to a European central bank, were made to the daily Helsingin Sanomat.</p>
<p>He expected the Dutch model of valuing gold reserves at 70 percent of the average market value over three years to be adopted by other countries, including Germany, the paper quoted him as saying.</p>
<p>Italy, the Netherlands, Belgium, Denmark, France and Austria all have similar regular revaluation procedures, an EU official said recently.</p>
